  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Complex Exponentials",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret $e^{j\\omega t}$ as a unit-magnitude complex number rotating in the complex plane at angular frequency $\\omega$, connecting Euler’s formula and polar form to sinusoidal phasor and Fourier representations.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Complex Exponentials",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A complex exponential like $e^{j\\theta}$ is a point on the unit circle written in polar form. Euler's formula tells you exactly how to read it: the real part is $\\cos\\theta$ and the imaginary part is $\\sin\\theta$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\theta}=\\cos\\theta+j\\sin\\theta",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That means the point never leaves the unit circle, because its magnitude is $\\sqrt{\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta}=1$. So the complex exponential has unit length even while its angle changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|e^{j\\omega t}|=\\sqrt{\\cos^2(\\omega t)+\\sin^2(\\omega t)}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now set $\\theta=\\omega t$. At $t=0$, $e^{j\\omega 0}=1$, so the phasor starts on the positive real axis. As $t$ increases, the angle advances to $\\omega t$, which means the point rotates continuously at angular frequency $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega t}=\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That rotating-point view is the one you will reuse later for phasors, complex amplitudes, and Fourier representations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing $e^{j\\omega t}$ as Unit Magnitude",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Show that $x(t)=e^{j4t}$ has magnitude 1 for every $t$ and identify what that means geometrically.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by rewriting $x(t)=e^{j4t}$ with Euler's formula. That gives the real part $\\cos(4t)$ and the imaginary part $\\sin(4t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j4t}=\\cos(4t)+j\\sin(4t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now compute the magnitude from those components: $|x(t)|=\\sqrt{\\cos^2(4t)+\\sin^2(4t)}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|x(t)|=\\sqrt{\\cos^2(4t)+\\sin^2(4t)}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the magnitude is always $1$, the point traced by $e^{j4t}$ stays on the unit circle. The factor $4$ changes the rotation rate, not the radius.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding the Angle of $e^{j\\omega t}$ at a Given Time",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{j2t}$, find the angle at $t=\\pi/4$ and state the corresponding position on the unit circle.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the exponent $j2t$: in $e^{j\\omega t}$, the angle is $\\theta=\\omega t$. So for this signal, set $\\theta=2t$ and substitute the given time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta=\\omega t=2\\cdot\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{\\pi}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "No reduction is needed here because $\\pi/2$ is already a standard angle. That means the phasor turns one quarter of a circle counterclockwise from the positive real axis.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=e^{j\\pi/2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the angle at $t=\\pi/4$ is $\\pi/2$, and the corresponding point on the unit circle is $j$ at the top of the circle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Locating $e^{j\\omega t}$ on the Unit Circle",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Locate $x(t)=e^{j\\pi/3}$ on the unit circle by writing its rectangular coordinates and naming the quadrant.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the exponential form $e^{j\\theta}$. By Euler's formula, that point on the unit circle has rectangular coordinates $(\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\theta}=\\cos\\theta+j\\sin\\theta",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the angle is $\\theta=\\pi/3$, so substitute that value into the cosine and sine. That gives the coordinates of the point directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\left(\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{3},\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Both coordinates are positive, so the point is in the first quadrant. In rectangular form, $x(t)=e^{j\\pi/3}=\\frac{1}{2}+j\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Angular Frequency as Rotation Rate",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compare $x_1(t)=e^{j2t}$ and $x_2(t)=e^{j5t}$ and determine which rotates faster and how much phase each accumulates by $t=\\pi/2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the coefficient of $t$ in $e^{j\\omega t}$. That coefficient is the angular rotation rate, so read off $\\omega_1=2$ and $\\omega_2=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta_1(t)=2t,\\qquad \\theta_2(t)=5t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because phase grows as $\\theta(t)=\\omega t$, the larger $|\\omega|$ turns faster. Here both rates are positive, so both signals rotate counterclockwise, but $x_2(t)$ advances more quickly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta_1\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=2\\cdot\\frac{\\pi}{2}=\\pi,\\qquad \\theta_2\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=5\\cdot\\frac{\\pi}{2}=\\frac{5\\pi}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So by $t=\\pi/2$, $x_1(t)$ has accumulated $\\pi$ radians and $x_2(t)$ has accumulated $5\\pi/2$ radians. The larger angular frequency gives the faster rotation, and a negative $\\omega$ would reverse direction without changing the speed rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
